## Installation

Install required Python libraries:

pip install pandas numpy networkx scipy matplotlib scikit-learn
## How to Run
Place dataset files in the correct directory
Update file paths in the script or notebook
Run the code in order:
Load graphs
Analyze graph structure
Generate features
Train model
Evaluate performance

## Methodology
Graph Construction
Load edge lists into NetworkX graphs
Support for both directed and undirected graphs
Edge weights represent repeated connections

## Graph Analysis

We compute:

Number of nodes and edges
Degree distributions
Connected components
Clustering coefficient
PageRank
Community detection using label propagation

## Feature Engineering

For each pair of nodes (u, v), the following features are computed:

Common Neighbors: Number of shared neighbors
Jaccard Coefficient: Ratio of shared neighbors to total neighbors
Adamic-Adar Index: Weighted importance of shared neighbors
Preferential Attachment: Product of node degrees
Same Community: Whether both nodes belong to the same detected community
Degree Features: In-degree and out-degree of nodes

## Negative Sampling

The dataset only contains existing edges (positive samples). To create a balanced dataset:

Positive samples: Existing edges

Negative samples: Randomly generated node pairs with no edge

This converts the problem into a binary classification task.

## Model Training

We use the following model:

Gaussian Naive Bayes

Steps:

Split dataset into training and testing sets (80/20)
Train the model on extracted features
Predict link existence on test data

## Evaluation Metrics

The model is evaluated using:

Accuracy
Precision
Recall
F1 Score
ROC-AUC
PR-AUC
Log Loss
Brier Score
Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC)
Cohen’s Kappa
Balanced Accuracy
NDCG (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain)
Precision@K

## Visualizations

The following plots are generated:

Degree distribution histograms
Component size distributions
Confusion matrix
ROC curve
Precision-Recall curve
Calibration curve

## Results

The model predicts whether a link exists between two nodes based on graph-derived features. Performance varies depending on the dataset and feature effectiveness.

## Limitations
Negative samples are randomly generated and may not represent realistic non-links
Gaussian Naive Bayes assumes feature independence
Real-world graphs are highly imbalanced, but this project uses a balanced dataset
Possible data leakage if edges are not split before feature computation

## Future Improvements
Use more advanced models:
Logistic Regression
